 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "Did Hannibal really march elephants over the Alps?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "He did. Around three dozen war elephants. And we still don't know which mountain pass he took."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Where did he start?",
   "scene": "route",
   "date": "218 BC",
   "place": "Cartagena, Spain"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In Spain, at Cartagena. It belonged to Carthage, a city in today's Tunisia, and Rome's great rival."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Why not go by sea?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Rome had beaten Carthage at sea in the first war. So in 218 BC, Hannibal went by land: over the Pyrenees, across southern France, and into the Alps.",
   "say": "Rome had beaten Carthage at sea in the first war. So in two eighteen BC, Hannibal went by land: over the Pyrenees, across southern France, and into the Alps."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "How do you get elephants across a river?",
   "scene": "rhone",
   "place": "River Rhône",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.56,
     0.45,
     1.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "At the Rhône, his men built huge rafts and covered them with soil, so the elephants thought they were still on solid ground."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And the mountains?",
   "scene": "alps",
   "date": "Autumn 218 BC",
   "place": "The Alps",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.55,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It was late autumn. Snow, ice, landslides, and mountain tribes attacking from above. The crossing took about fifteen days."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "How many men made it?",
   "scene": "count"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "After the Pyrenees, he had about fifty-nine thousand. He came down into Italy with twenty-six thousand."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And the elephants?",
   "scene": "elephants"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Most died in the first winter. Hannibal rode the last one through flooded marshes, and lost an eye to infection."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Was it worth it?",
   "scene": "italy",
   "place": "Italy"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "For Rome, it was a nightmare. He beat three Roman armies in three years."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "At Cannae, he surrounded a much bigger Roman army. About fifty thousand Romans died in a single day.",
   "scene": "cannae",
   "date": "216 BC",
   "place": "Cannae, Italy",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.55,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.48,
     0.6,
     1.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So why didn't Rome fall?",
   "scene": "zama",
   "date": "202 BC",
   "place": "Zama, Tunisia"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Hannibal never besieged the city. Rome kept raising new armies, then invaded Africa. In 202 BC, at Zama, Scipio finally beat him.",
   "say": "Hannibal never besieged the city. Rome kept raising new armies, then invaded Africa. In two oh-two BC, at Zama, Scipio finally beat him."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So which pass did he take?",
   "scene": "mystery"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Nobody knows for sure. One team found a thick layer of ancient horse dung, dated to Hannibal's time, below a high pass called the Col de la Traversette.",
   "say": "Nobody knows for sure. One team found a thick layer of ancient horse dung, dated to Hannibal's time, below a high pass called the Coll de la Traverset."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Next time: Rome wipes Carthage off the map. Like the video and follow History Sketches so you don't miss it."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And tell us in the comments: would you have crossed the Alps?"
  },
  {
   "type": "end",
   "text": "Next: Carthage",
   "sub": "Follow History Sketches"
  }
 ]
